{
"A traveling poet who criticized the human-shaped gods of Homer and Hesiod and noticed that every people makes its gods in its own image.": "1554246bd9e6",
"Anaximander corrected Thales and Anaximenes corrected Anaximander. A school in which students are expected to improve on their teacher, by giving reasons, is the seed of every later science and philosophy.": "831669e1a420",
"Anaximenes said that air turns to water when it condenses.": "ba376eb08547",
"As is ordained; for they make reparation and satisfaction to one another for their injustice according to the appointed time.": "0175dc17f442",
"Chose air, and explained the other substances by thinning and condensing. His idea that change in kind comes from change in density is one of the first physical mechanisms.": "76622777f52b",
"Counted by Aristotle as the founder of natural philosophy. Said that the principle of all things is water, and remembered for predicting an eclipse, cornering the olive presses, and falling into a well.": "48d96acca4ec",
"Every culture has an account of how the world began. The Greek poet Hesiod, writing around 700 BCE, gave one in his Theogony, the birth of the gods. In H. G. Evelyn-Whites translation, it begins: Verily at the first Chaos came to be, but next wide-bosomed Earth, the ever-sure foundations of all. Earth then gives birth to Heaven, and Heaven and Earth produce the next generation of gods, who quarrel, plot, and overthrow their parents. In this kind of account, the world is explained the way a family is explained, by who was born from whom and who fought whom. Thunder happens because Zeus is angry. The sea is rough because Poseidon wills it. The explanation is a story about persons, and it cannot be checked except by trusting the poet who tells it.": "4ce9173e9114",
"Hesiods cosmology was a family story of gods.": "82e743128115",
"Historians reconstruct early Greek thought from later reports.": "40940f0626d9",
"If oxen and horses or lions had hands, and could paint with their hands, and produce works of art as men do, horses would paint the forms of the gods like horses, and oxen like oxen.": "c18df70a4293",
"Many sayings are attributed to Thales, but none survive in his own words.": "88a826387015",
"Philosophy, as the Greeks later told it, began about a century after Hesiod in Miletus, a wealthy trading city on the coast of Ionia, in what is now western Turkey. Miletus had ships in Egypt and colonies around the Black Sea, and it lived next door to the Lydian kingdom and within reach of Babylon. Its merchants met people with other gods and other stories, and saw that the stories did not agree. They also had access to Egyptian methods of measuring land and to centuries of Babylonian records of the stars. A city like this had leisure, money, and a reason to wonder which account of the world, if any, was true.": "bd9d64199524",
"Proposed the apeiron, the boundless, as the source of everything, and left the oldest surviving sentence of Greek philosophy. Also credited with an early map of the world.": "8e54cea44d69",
"Thales of Miletus left no writings, and everything we know about him comes from authors who lived long after him. The most important is Aristotle, writing about two and a half centuries later. In W. D. Rosss translation of the Metaphysics: Thales, the founder of this type of philosophy, says the principle is water (for which reason he declared that the earth rests on water). Aristotle then guesses at his reasons, getting the notion perhaps from seeing that the nutriment of all things is moist, and that the seeds of all things have a moist nature. Notice the word perhaps. Even Aristotle was reconstructing. But the shape of the claim is clear. Behind the variety of things there is one basic stuff, and the world is explained by what that stuff does, not by what a god wants.": "1593db2e2fa7",
"Thales said that the principle of all things is water.": "a0624ef9bce5",
"Thales, the founder of this type of philosophy, says the principle is water (for which reason he declared that the earth rests on water).": "31e23cceaf0e",
"The Milesian theories were speculative, but they invited argument.": "1861ed44991d",
"The anecdote of the olive presses shows Thales as a shrewd investor.": "eb6043432a44",
"The poet-philosopher Xenophanes, from another Ionian city, turned the new habit of criticism on the gods themselves. In Burnets translation: if oxen and horses or lions had hands, and could paint with their hands, and produce works of art as men do, horses would paint the forms of the gods like horses, and oxen like oxen. The Ethiopians make their gods black and snub-nosed, he added, and the Thracians give theirs blue eyes and red hair. The old phrase for all this is from myth to reason, and it needs care. Thales himself is reported to have said that all things are full of gods, the Milesians borrowed from Babylonian and Egyptian learning, and myth went on living beside philosophy for a thousand years. What changed was not that people stopped believing in gods. It was that a new kind of explanation appeared, one that could be argued with.": "9970b3aa88e0",
"The question of what single thing underlies the many, the archē, set the agenda for centuries. The atomists, Plato, and Aristotle all answered it in different ways, and the next lessons follow them.": "0b9715f16914",
"Three stories about Thales show how the Greeks remembered him. Herodotus reports that during a battle between the Lydians and the Medes suddenly the day became night, and that Thales had foretold this change. Modern astronomers date a solar eclipse visible there to 585 BCE, though historians doubt he could have predicted it so precisely. Aristotle tells that when people mocked Thales poverty as proof that philosophy is useless, he foresaw a large olive harvest and rented all the olive presses in advance, to show that philosophers can easily be rich if they like, but that their ambition is of another sort. And Plato tells the opposite story, of a clever witty Thracian handmaid who laughed when Thales fell into a well as he was looking up at the stars. The absent-minded professor and the shrewd investor are both born here.": "54518bdf8e6e",
"Thunder and floods were no longer explained by a gods mood but by what water, air, or the boundless does. The Greeks called this subject physis, nature, and the word survives in physics.": "613bf52cd9df",
"What made Miletus a beginning was not Thales alone but the people after him. Anaximander, said to be his student, rejected water. The basic stuff, he argued, could not be any one of the familiar elements, since each is the opposite of another and would have overwhelmed the rest. He called it the apeiron, the boundless. One sentence of his survives, in John Burnets translation: things pass away into that from which they arose, as is ordained; for they make reparation and satisfaction to one another for their injustice according to the appointed time. Anaximenes, the next generation, proposed air, which becomes fire when thinned and wind, cloud, water, and stone when condensed. Here, for the first time, a teachers view was openly corrected by his students, with reasons, and the correction was treated as progress.": "fd5884b366b2",
"Xenophanes mocked the anthropomorphic gods of Homer.": "8400bea6a1d0",
